3.2. Training and Evaluation The training, development, and test corpora for both languages were assembled by crawling the web (Scannell, 2007). The corpora are sentence-shufﬂed,4 tokenized, and then lowercased. It is important to take note of a subtlety when lowercasing Irish and Scottish Gaelic that involves initial mutations. When t-prothesis or eclipsis occurs with a capitalized vowel-initial word in Irish, the preﬁxed letter is written without a hyphen, e.g. a´r nAthair (‘our Father’).
